# tcpdrill, the RFC 7323 window scale option.
#
# NEEDS A BUILD THAT HAS THE OPTION, which is the default.  Against a library
# built with -DAMINETXDUO_TCP_WINDOW_SCALING=OFF the stack offers nothing and
# x01 fails on its first line.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS.  The option is three bytes and the word carrying it is
# four, and the constant that fills that word put the pad byte BEHIND the
# option: kind 3, length 3, shift, zero.  A zero byte in a TCP option area is
# an end-of-option-list, so every option the SYN appends after the window
# scale -- SACK-Permitted, the timestamp -- sat past the end of the list.  They
# were on the wire and no conforming parser could reach them.  Nothing failed:
# the connection came up, the window scaled, and the two options the peer never
# saw simply did not happen.
#
# x01 IS THAT CASE.  The harness decoder stops at an EOL exactly as a peer
# does, so asserting the three options together on one SYN is the assertion,
# and `hdrlen=auto` adds that the data offset accounts for those options and
# nothing else -- an option area with room for something unreachable in it
# fails here too.
#
# Conventions are tcp.drill's.  Two more:
#   * `win`, `winmin` and `winmax` are the receiver's window with the scale
#     already taken out, so a case says what the receiver has room for and not
#     what fits in sixteen bits.
#   * `$rwnd` is that window measured on the first frame after the handshake,
#     which is RCV.BUFF: no case here contains a window the build chose.
#
# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT

# The layout, all of it at once.  RFC 7323 2.2 for the scale, RFC 2018 section
# 2 for SACK-Permitted, RFC 7323 3.2 for the timestamp: a SYN this stack sends
# offers all three, so all three have to be readable by something that walks
# the option area properly.
case x01_syn_carries_scale_sack_and_timestamps
socket
connect
tx S seq=0 mss=1460 wsok=1 sackok=1 ts=1 hdrlen=auto

# RFC 7323 2.2: the window in a SYN is never scaled, so what leaves is the
# buffer capped at 65535 whatever the build's ceiling is.  $synwin is that cap.
case x02_the_syn_window_is_unscaled
socket
connect
tx S seq=0 winmin=$winfloor winmax=$synwin

# RFC 7323 2.2: a SYN-ACK offers the scale only when the peer's SYN did.  This
# peer offered all three, so all three come back.
case x03_passive_open_repeats_the_offer
localport 4741
listen
rx S seq=0 win=8192 mss=1460 wscale=2 sackok=1 tsval=1000
tx SA seq=0 ack=1 mss=1460 wsok=1 sackok=1 ts=1 tsecr=1000 hdrlen=auto
rx A seq=1 ack=1 tsval=1001
accept

# The same, from a peer that said nothing.  Offering a scale to a peer that did
# not ask for one leaves the two ends reading the same sixteen bits
# differently, which is the failure RFC 7323 2.2 is written to prevent.
case x04_passive_open_silent_when_the_peer_was
localport 4742
listen
rx S seq=0 win=8192 mss=1460
tx SA seq=0 ack=1 mss=1460 wsok=0 sackok=0 ts=0 hdrlen=auto
rx A seq=1 ack=1
accept

# RFC 7323 2.2 again: our SYN offered, the peer's SYN-ACK did not, so the
# option is off in BOTH directions and the window has to fit the field.  A
# stack that kept scaling here would advertise a window sixteen times what it
# has.
case x05_a_declining_peer_takes_our_scale_with_it
socket
connect
tx S seq=0 wsok=1
rx SA seq=0 ack=1 win=8192 mss=1460
tx A seq=1 ack=1 winmax=65535
send 20
tx PA seq=1 ack=1 len=20 winmax=65535

# The window update, on a connection that DID settle a scale.  Same shape as
# rwndupdate.drill s03 and the same derivation -- the step is RCV.BUFF/2 and
# the test is `>=`, so $rwnd/2-1/1460 reads may be silent and the next must
# carry the update -- with the whole exchange running in scaled units.  This is
# the arithmetic a shift breaks if the window is announced in the wrong one.
case x06_the_window_update_is_carried_scaled
socket
connect
tx S seq=0 wsok=1
rx SA seq=0 ack=1 win=32768 mss=1460 wscale=0
tx A seq=1 ack=1
repeat $rwnd/2-1/1460+2
rx PA seq=$i*1460+1 ack=1 len=1460
tx A seq=1 ack=$i*1460+1461 within=400
end
repeat $rwnd/2-1/1460
recv 1460 = 1460
end
notx 300
recv 1460 = 1460
tx A seq=1 ack=$rwnd/2-1/1460*1460+2921 win=$rwnd-1460 within=900
recv 1460 = 1460
notx 300
